#ident	"@(#)gen++:d.pkg/README	1.19"

This directory contains RELEASE 1.2 of gen++, a facility for building
tools for C++ programs.
It is based on GENOA -- "GENerator Of Analyzers", a language independent
tool generator, and is currently tied to the C++ Translator (cfront)
release 3.0.3.  (This software is compatible with the C++ Translator
from USL but is not guaranteed to work with other C++ compilation systems,
such as g++.)

Machines:
	This package has been ported to Sun 4.* and hpux.  Other machines
	will be supported as demand requires. 

This release also contains source and documentation for the OO rule checker
and the MIT OO metrics suite, implemented using gen++.  See subdirectories
rc/ and metrics/. 

Files:
	README
	README.nmake  - nmake rules for use with gen++ analyzers
	RELEASE.notes - contains notes on the current release
	makefile      - builds analyzer from query (see below)
	mk            - small script to simplify running makefile
	examples/*    - contains sample queries
	rc/*          - source and documentation for the OO rule checker
	metrics/*     - source and documentation for the OO metrics suite
	docs/abstract.ps - a postscript version of a short paper introducing
	                gen++
	docs/gen++.ps - contains a postscript version of a draft gen++ manual
		        Note: This memo contains copies of the genii spec and
		        many of the sample queries and therefore appears to
		        be longer than it really is.
	docs/type.summary - ascii file summarizing the node type hierarchy
			in the GENII spec and providing a brief description
			for each type
	docs/specfile - ascii file giving a detailed summary of node types
			and slots, needed to write queries.  (This informa-
			tion is also included in the manual appendix.)
	docs/typesummary.html -
		        This file has the same information as type.summary
		        above, except that it is in a form that can be read
     		        by Mosaic (or similar WWW/HTML browser). 
	docs/typedocs.html -
		        This file (which is indexed by the above file)
		        gives information about the different types,
		        and the slots and fillers for each type. 
		        The two *.html files provide the same information
		        as in the appendix of the manual, but in
		        user-friendly hypertext form.
	*.[hoa],cplang
	              - supporting software needed to build analyzers
	GENPP.dat     - license file -- referenced by all executables 
	QCC           - script used to run the generated tools

Installation:
	mk and makefile were designed to run from any directory, but reference
	files in the installation directory.  To install, simply copy *.[hoa],
	cplang and GENPP.dat to a directory D of your choice and edit mk and
	makefile accordingly. 
	On systems that use ranlib, DON'T FORGET TO RUN
		ranlib $D/gfront.a
	before running any builds.


To Build an Analyzer:
	makefile and mk are designed to run from any directory when configured
	properly.  Copy makefile to your private directory and edit it
	to set the first 5 macros to appropriate values for your machine
	/ application:
		CPL = c++ compiler
		CC  = c compiler
		GEN = path name of gen++ installation directory
		OTHERLIBS = additional libraries / .o's specific to your
				application.
		OTHERINCLUDES = additional preprocessor options specific
				to your application
	These macros can alternatively be set in the mk script.  mk 
	exports info needed by the license manager and also simplifies 
	using makefile. 
	Copy and edit mk as needed.
	For example, set options on the make line,
	or set OTHERLIBS and OTHERINCLUDES at the top.
	(SEE BELOW FOR DETAILS ON LICENSE MANAGER.)

	Place a gen++ specification in a file myspec.query
	and execute
		mk myspec
	This will build an executable called
		analyzer
	which accepts the same command line and input as cfront but
	replaces code generation by code which implements your query. 
	OTHERLIBS specifies libraries to be linked with queries which
	reference external functions.  See the manual for details.

	Note: This process generates intermediate files with fixed names, 
	so that two analyzer builds can't run simultaneously in the same 
	directory.

	IMPORTANT: If two different analyzers are built in the same directory,
	it is important to run
		make clean
	before building the second to clear away any intermediate files
	specific to the first.  It is not necessary to run 'make clean'
	between consecutive builds of the same analyzer.

To Run:
	The analyzer produced by mk could be substituted for cfront
	in the CC shell script, except that it currently generates no
	code, so C compilation and linking will fail.
	(It is also possible that a user will write arbitrary output to stdout,
	which would not be legal C.)
	Also, due to the way temp files are handled in CC, the output
	from an analyzer may be lost.  For example, if a query sends output
	to a specially named file, such as query.out, this file will be
	created in /usr/tmp, and removed by CC.

	To avoid this, edit and use the QCC script provided for running
	analyzers.  QCC will use CC to preprocess a file and run
	the analyzer in the current directory, sending output to stdout.

	For all scripts,  be sure ksh is available on your system.

License Manager:
	Beginning with release 1.2, a license manager is incorporated into
	cplang and the analyzers built using gen++.  These reference the
	environment variable
		GENPP_LICENSE_FILE
	which must be exported and set to the path name of the license file
	GENPP.dat included in the installation directory.   Note that this
	is done in mk, QCC, rc/RC and rc/mk.  Be sure to edit these scripts
	appropriately to set all relevant path names.

Sample Queries:
	Sample queries are also provided in the examples directory.
	For example, to build an analyzer from examples/hier.query, type
		mk examples/hier
	Details about each query can be found in the comments.

	As more elaborate examples of gen++ applications, this
	release also includes the OO Rule Checker and an OO
	metrics suite (found in directories rc/ and metrics/,
	respectively).  Documentation is included in associated
	README files.

	Enjoy!
